    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and `pytest -q` passes (2 tests: the existing status test and one new test).\n\n**One thing you should know:** the pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` is built on `httpx2`, not `httpx`. It raises a `TypeError` if you give it an `httpx` client, so I couldn't pass it `build_http_client()` from `app/http.py`. Instead, `build_claude` creates the SDK's own client (`DefaultHttpxClient`) and fills it from what `app/http.py` already defines:\n- **Header:** `X-Egress-Tag` set to `EGRESS_TAG`.\n- **Logging:** `log_egress` as the request hook. The hook runs on every attempt, so retries are logged too.\n- **Timeouts:** built from `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` and `READ_TIMEOUT`. They're set on the Claude client as well, because the SDK applies its own timeout to each request and would otherwise use its 10-minute default.\n- **Transport:** the `transport` you pass in is used by the HTTP client.\n- **Retries:** `max_retries=2`.\n\nI didn't change `app/http.py` or `fetch_status`. The one duplicated line is the header and hook settings, which also appear in `build_http_client`. The header name and the numbers themselves still come only from `app/http.py`.\n\nBecause of this, the stub transport your tests pass must be an `httpx2` transport (e.g. `httpx2.MockTransport`). An `httpx` one won't work.\n\nI added `tests/test_claude.py`. Its stub transport fails the first request with a 500 and then succeeds. The test checks that both attempts carry the header, use the 5s connect and 60s read timeouts, and each produce one `POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages` line on `app.egress`."
  },
